EXPERIMENT 2 — REVERSE IMAGE SEARCH AND GEOLOCATION
================================================================

PART A — PRACTICAL PERFORMANCE

TOOLS

Google Lens:
https://lens.google.com

TinEye:
https://tineye.com

Google Maps:
https://maps.google.com

Metadata2Go:
https://www.metadata2go.com


OPTIONAL KALI LINUX METADATA COMMANDS

sudo apt update

sudo apt install libimage-exiftool-perl -y

exiftool image.jpg

exiftool -GPSLatitude -GPSLongitude image.jpg


Replace image.jpg with the actual image filename.


STEPS

1. Save the given image or use its provided URL.

2. Upload the image to Google Lens or TinEye.

3. Check matching images and possible original sources.

4. Record the source URLs and dates.

5. Inspect visual clues:

   - Road signs and street names

   - Buildings and landmarks

   - Road markings and traffic signs

   - Mountains, coastlines and terrain

   - Language on signboards and shops

   - Shadows and environmental features

6. Compare the clues with Google Maps or Street View.

7. If the original image is available, inspect it using Metadata2Go or ExifTool.

8. Check metadata fields:

   - Date and time

   - Camera/device make and model

   - Image dimensions

   - GPS latitude and longitude

   - Software used to edit the image

9. If GPS coordinates exist, enter them into a map.

10. Cross-check at least two visual clues before reporting the likely location.


IMPORTANT

Metadata may be missing or removed. Do not guess a location when the evidence is insufficient.


PART B — THEORY TO WRITE ON PAPER

BACKGROUND / THEORY (50–100 WORDS)

Reverse image search is an OSINT technique used to find matching images, identify possible original sources and discover where an image has appeared online. Geolocation estimates the place shown in an image by examining landmarks, road signs, buildings, terrain and metadata. EXIF metadata may contain the capture date, device details and GPS coordinates, but it may be absent or removed. Visual clues and metadata should therefore be cross-checked before concluding the likely location.


OPEN-SOURCE TOOL (AROUND 50 WORDS)

Google Lens and TinEye help identify visually similar images and possible source webpages. Google Maps helps compare landmarks and streets. ExifTool is an open-source command-line utility that extracts image metadata, including GPS fields when present. These tools help investigate image origins and locations, but results need independent verification.


PERFORMANCE STEPS

1. Upload the image to Google Lens or TinEye.

2. Record matching images and source URLs.

3. Inspect landmarks, signs and road features.

4. Check metadata using Metadata2Go or ExifTool.

5. Compare GPS data and visual clues with maps.

6. Record the likely source or location with evidence.


CONCLUSION

The image was investigated using reverse image search, metadata and visual clues to identify its possible source or location.



